     8	# Empirical Truth Battery: executable LLM/machine partition
     9	
    10	## 0. Authority, merge law, and status
    11	
    12	This document answers the mission question operationally: deterministic code should own state, execution, validation, ledgers, arithmetic, routing, and enforcement; LLMs should receive only bounded semantic decisions that cannot honestly be computed from typed state. The partition preserves all 62 battery questions and all nine human rules while minimizing repeated context and model-generated bookkeeping.
    13	
    14	This document does **not** claim that the proposed battery, the partition, or the savings work in production. The upstream plan calls the battery an unrun candidate, records zero end-to-end executions of its contested machinery, and reports no matched-cost comparison (plan Parts 8–10).
    15	
    16	Normative terms:
    17	
    18	- `MUST`, `MUST NOT`, `SHOULD`, and `MAY` have their ordinary requirements meaning.
    19	- `MACHINE`: after required typed inputs exist, no runtime LLM judgment belongs to this row.
    20	- `LLM`: the row's substantive result is an irreducible semantic judgment; code may still persist, validate schema, and enforce consequences.
    21	- `HYBRID`: deterministic computation materially narrows, checks, or acts on a minimal LLM judgment.
    22	- `CONTESTED`: the seat verdicts differ. It is not a fourth implementation technique and must not be compiled to a winner until an authorized later round adjudicates it.
