    38	    unanimous_machine: 0
    39	    unanimous_llm: 0
    40	    unanimous_hybrid: 5
    41	    contested: 4
    42	  total_rows: 71
    43	  total_contested: 28
    44	```
    45	
    46	For every contested row, the `Verdict` cell names `Hermes`, `Codex`, and `Grok` on one line. The remaining columns specify the common execution envelope found across the seats; they do not settle whether that envelope is best named LLM, HYBRID, or MACHINE. An implementation MAY build that common envelope, but production activation of the disputed boundary requires an explicit adjudication record.
    47	
    48	Grounding syntax is `Plan Part N / Stage N Qx`; question IDs follow the plan appendix (`Q1`–`Q62`), and human-rule IDs follow Part 4 (`R1`–`R9`). The stage-local question number is omitted where the stable global ID is clearer.
    49	
    50	## 0A. V RULING (2026-08-03) — The governing question: the whole-graph stranger test
    51	
    52	This section is a V-directed policy amendment recorded after the Round-2 handoffs.
    53	It does not alter any seat's verdict; it extends the scope of `R9` and binds the
    54	purpose of the entire preflight design. V designates the following as **the crux
    55	of preflight**: every deterministic structure this document specifies exists so
    56	that this one question can be answered "yes."
    57	
    58	> **"Could the person who asked me this — knowing nothing about how I work —
    59	> read ALL NODES AND THE VERDICT and correctly tell someone else what the answer
    60	> is, how sure I am, what would change my mind, and what they should now do
    61	> differently?"**
    62	
    63	Normative effects:
    64	
    65	1. **`R9` scope extension.** The stranger test applies to the ENTIRE argument
    66	   graph, not only the served top layer. Every generated node — each child,
    67	   defeater, and residual sentence — MUST individually be human-readable and
    68	   restatable by a stranger. The audit panel (hashes, tags, version constants)
    69	   remains below the fold, but node *text* is human language, always. Wherever
    70	   this document's contracts reference R9, read them with this scope.
    71	2. **Generation-time constraint on `Q26`/`Q27`.** The smallest-LLM-output
    72	   schemas for children, defeaters, and residuals gain a binding output
    73	   requirement: node text MUST be phrased in plain human language at generation
    74	   time. This is a prompt-level constraint on existing calls; it adds no calls.
    75	3. **`Q28` gains a readability dimension.** The isolated-context test asks not
    76	   only "can this node be answered cold" but "can it be *said back* cold" —
    77	   the fresh context restates the node's meaning, and code diffs the
    78	   restatement. A node that cannot be restated is defective even if answerable.
    79	4. **Enforcement stays MACHINE.** Fresh-context restatement, field comparison,
    80	   and block-on-mismatch are deterministic; only the restating call itself is
    81	   LLM. Rewrite the node, never educate the reader.
    82	5. **New unresolved human parameter** (joins Section 6; no default permitted):
    83	
    84	```ts
    85	strangerTestCoverage: Unresolved; // owner: V
    86	// expectedType: "exhaustive_all_nodes" | "load_bearing_nodes_only"
    87	//             | "sampled(rate)" — per-node restatement calls scale with
    88	//             node count; V prices this, implementation never defaults it.
    89	```
    90	
    91	Rationale (V's words, condensed): people read each node we generate, not only
    92	the first and last reasoning nodes. The question exists to make sure humans
    93	understand our answers; a graph whose nodes read as machine dialect fails the
    94	mission even when the verdict passes.
    95	
    96	## 1. Consolidated execution architecture
    97	
    98	### 1.1 Global invariants
    99	
   100	1. Preserve the original question byte-for-byte; normalization lives in a separate field.
   101	2. Every semantic field starts `UNKNOWN`, never `0.5`, empty-string-as-answer, or a guessed policy value.
   102	3. No retrieval begins before LOCK and the query-plan gate permit it.
   103	4. Retrieval, measurement execution, arithmetic, counters, diffs, persistence, and enforcement run outside the LLM.
   104	5. Research and criticism never share a context; an artifact's producer never grades it (plan Stage 8 law).
   105	6. An LLM never receives the full accumulated dossier when a smaller projection suffices. Calls exchange stable IDs and unresolved fields.
   106	7. Rereading without new external input may lower confidence, widen a range, or abstain; it may not raise confidence or narrow a range (plan Part 1).
   107	8. Every load-bearing claim has exactly one epistemic kind—`LOOKED_UP`, `RAN`, or `REASONING`—plus producer and locator/run reference. Missing provenance blocks serving (plan Part 1 and Q51).
   108	9. A machine result is evidence only when its run provenance, reproducibility, and instrument validation satisfy Stage 5.
   109	10. Cache hits reduce work but never certify truth. Policy, evidence cutoff, source version, or dependency changes invalidate affected descendants.
   110	11. `CONTESTED` rows remain visibly unresolved; no majority inference, label normalization, or “mostly agreed” shortcut is permitted.
   111	12. Every gate must be tested to fire both ways before adoption (plan Part 5 agreement 12).
   112	
   113	### 1.2 Order of operations
   114	
   115	The merged design separates a true one-time preflight from later deterministic stage compilers. This preserves Hermes and Codex's strict “before any LLM call” boundary while incorporating Grok's useful machine-first work at each stage boundary without calling every such pass “preflight.”
   116	
   117	| Order | Component | Deterministic work | LLM work | Output / stop |
   118	|---:|---|---|---|---|
   119	| P0 | Intake | Preserve original; canonicalize transport separately; mint `run_id`; record caller, `received_at`, `as_of`, locale/jurisdiction if supplied. | None. | Reject malformed required transport fields. |
   120	| P1 | Policy injection | Load versioned V-owned decisions, rule approvals, lineage relation, registries, operator/scoring versions, limits, and serve layout. | None. | Any required unresolved policy remains `UNRESOLVED`; code MUST NOT invent a default. |
   121	| P2 | Resource snapshot | Enumerate retrievers, data, tools, runners, persistence, quota, time, network, owners, eligible critic lineages, and deterministic marginal cost. | None. | Impossible mandatory capability becomes a typed blocker, not a prompt. |
   122	| P3 | Identity/cache | Hash original/canonical question plus policy and evidence-cutoff versions; load exact reusable artifacts and calibration/liveness records. | None. | No semantic cache hit may be inferred from text similarity alone. |
   123	| P4 | Literal extraction | Extract dates, numbers, units, quoted strings, explicit options, URLs/identifiers, and caller-supplied scope fields. | None. | These are literals, not inferred topic, scope, type, domain, value, or presupposition. |
   124	| P5 | Integrity | Validate hashes, timestamps, signatures, schema versions, locator syntax, references, and replayable arithmetic. | None. | Mark artifacts `VALID`, `STALE`, `POLICY_CHANGED`, `MISSING`, or `CORRUPT`. |
   125	| P6 | State initialization | Create typed ledgers, stable ID namespaces, append-only events, token/retrieval/tool/retry counters, and `UNKNOWN` semantic slots. | None. | Malformed schema/config fails closed before model spend. |
   126	| P7 | Provisional activation | Compile every row predicate; activate only pre-semantic prerequisites and leave semantic predicates `UNKNOWN`. | None. | Never equate the source's inconsistent “always” marks with unconditional runtime activation. |
   127	| P8 | First prompt projection | Project original question, caller fields, literals, valid reusable references, policy enums, capabilities, and exact output schema. | None. | Persist immutable preflight envelope and `LLM_CALL_PENDING`. |
   128	| T1 | LOCK + R6 | Run Q1–Q6/R6 compilers and minimal semantic calls. | Intent, topic/scope, presuppositions, answer rule, prior anchor, residual feasibility judgment. | Stop on inert, false assumption, ill-posed, unaffordable, or surface ambiguity as specified. |
   129	| T2 | ROUTE + R7 | Load type/domain obligation registries and route enums. | Settlement act, question type/domain, live alternatives, split decision. | Value choice routes to human; compound question splits; unresolved type visibly defaults only if V has approved such policy. |
   130	| T3 | AIM + R1/R3/R4/R8 | Freeze/version queries; initialize ignorance/source/critic records; enforce source-class requirements. | Query language, unknowns, holders/interests, vantage points, hit criteria. | Empty query plan blocks retrieval; missing measurement/opposition classes cause visible downgrade. |
   131	| T4 | HARVEST | Execute admitted queries; cache source versions/spans; write search/absence logs; cluster provenance; compute ages. | Only ambiguous primary status, semantic support, volatility, and uncertain cluster edges. | Unrun query means incomplete; preview-only evidence cannot supply quote/number. |
   132	| T5 | RUN | Enumerate/execute probes; capture raw output; replay; run fixtures; preserve attempts. | Select smallest answer-moving probe, set pre-run prediction/falsifier, explain uncatalogued blocker/limit. | Irreproducible output becomes `REASONING`; no runnable probe causes documents-only record. |
   133	| T6 | SPLIT | Enter only when Q10 says split; enforce cap, isolation, retry, kill reasons, set comparisons, and later sensitivity. | Children/defeaters, residual sentence, falsifiers, alternate split. | Empty defeaters retry then rotate lineage then abstain; topic list is discarded; unresolved coverage stays visible. |
   134	| T7 | WEIGH | Pre-filter scope, compare symmetry, apply rubrics, compute intervals/sensitivities, retain ledgers. | Borderline relevance, diagnosticity, study bias, non-estimable uncertainty. | Unsupported/biased/non-diagnostic items are rejected, bounded, or zero-weighted as specified. |
   135	| T8 | CROSS + R5 | Build blinded delta packet; verify lineage/receipt; exact-check quotes; rerun sums; maintain objections. | Different-lineage semantic critique and unresolved support judgments. | Invalid/empty review reruns; strong unresolved objection blocks confident band; citation deviations change claims. |
   136	| T9 | COMPOSE | Execute declared operators, leverage, variants, matched-regime comparison, fragility, Pareto/rank stability. | Only disputed operator/criteria/dependence semantics. | Undeclared operator withholds parent number; flips are served, never averaged away. |
   137	| T10 | SERVE + R9 | Compile provenance, objection, movement, abstention, value separation, cutoff, and stranger-test fields. | Minimal wording/narrowing, normative-language judgment, revision trigger, fresh-context restatement. | Missing locator/visible objection/stranger-test match blocks serve; other failures downgrade or rewrite. |
   138	| T11 | SETTLE | Persist/read back answer; schedule resolution; ingest outcomes; proper-score; update calibration and liveness. | Resolver identification and ambiguous causal error attribution only. | Missing resolver marks permanently unscoreable; ignored resolution makes future calibrated confidence unavailable. |
   139	
   140	### 1.3 Architecture divergences that remain visible
   141	
   142	| ID | Divergence | Seat/source positions | Required treatment |
   143	|---|---|---|---|
   144	| ARCH-D1 | Meaning of “preflight” | Hermes and Codex: exactly once before any LLM and non-semantic. Grok: “once per question (or once per stage boundary)” and includes minimal stage LLM fills. | Use `PreflightState` only for P0–P8; call later passes stage compilers. This is terminology consolidation, not semantic adjudication. |
   145	| ARCH-D2 | Retrieve/measure before split | All three architecture sketches order HARVEST/RUN before SPLIT, but the plan says the ordering is genuinely contested: two seats treat it as design, two as sequence not law, two retain older order. | Treat T4→T5→T6 as an experimental default only if V supplies `ordering_policy`; validate split-first versus retrieve-first before making it law (plan Parts 5 and 7). |
   146	| ARCH-D3 | Activation truth | Source markers imply 43 “always” rows; the cost table implies roughly 13/40/48/45/44/7 by type; at least seven “always” rows are conditional inside their stage. | Row predicates below are the executable candidate graph. Do not claim the contradiction resolved until real-run activation is measured and an authoritative graph is approved (plan Parts 1 and 9). |
   147	| ARCH-D4 | No-critic enforcement | Plan records label-and-proceed, hold-provisional, and block-confident-band positions; seats often use the merged middle or combine middle/strict consequences. | Leave `critic_unavailable_policy` unresolved. Receipts and single-lineage status are always machine-computed; product enforcement awaits V. |
   148	| ARCH-D5 | Semantic caching | Codex expressly forbids inferred semantic hits from similarity; Hermes allows exact prior-run artifacts with invalidation; Grok emphasizes ID reuse. | Cache only exact artifacts keyed by content/version/policy/cutoff. Similarity may propose candidates but never fills semantic state or certifies evidence. |
   149	
   150	### 1.4 Typed state
   151	
   152	```ts
   153	type Id = string;
   154	type ISODateTime = string;
   155	type UnknownOr<T> = { status: "UNKNOWN" } | { status: "KNOWN"; value: T };
   156	type ArtifactValidity = "VALID" | "STALE" | "POLICY_CHANGED" | "MISSING" | "CORRUPT";
   157	type EpistemicKind = "LOOKED_UP" | "RAN" | "REASONING";
   158	type AbstentionKind =
   159	  | "NOT_SEARCHED" | "SEARCHED_ABSENT" | "MEASURED_INCONCLUSIVE"
   160	  | "NOT_RUNNABLE" | "VALUE_CHOICE";
   161	
   162	interface PreflightState {
   163	  run: {
   164	    runId: Id; originalText: string; canonicalText: string; questionHash: string;
   165	    receivedAt: ISODateTime; asOf: ISODateTime; callerId?: Id;
   166	    locale?: string; jurisdiction?: string;
   167	  };
   168	  policy: {
   169	    version: Id; values: HumanPolicyState; questionSpecVersion: Id;
   170	    operatorRegistryVersion: Id; rubricRegistryVersion: Id; scoringRegistryVersion: Id;
   171	  };
   172	  literals: {
   173	    dates: string[]; numbers: number[]; units: string[]; quotedStrings: string[];
   174	    identifiers: string[]; explicitOptions: string[]; callerScope: Record<string, unknown>;
   175	  };
   176	  resources: {
   177	    retrievers: Capability[]; instruments: Capability[]; data: Capability[];
   178	    persistence: Capability; quota: QuotaState; owners: OwnerRef[];
   179	    researcherLineage: LineageRef; criticCandidates: LineageRef[];
   180	  };
   181	  cache: {
   182	    artifacts: ArtifactRef[]; validityByArtifact: Record<Id, ArtifactValidity>;
   183	    calibrationRows: ArtifactRef[]; livenessRows: ArtifactRef[];
   184	  };
   185	  activation: {
   186	    graphVersion: Id; active: string[]; inactive: string[];
   187	    unknownPredicates: string[];
   188	  };
   189	  budgets: {
   190	    tokenCeiling?: number; callsRemaining?: number; retriesRemaining?: number;
   191	    machineCosts: Record<string, number>;
   192	  };
   193	  receipts: {
   194	    configHash: string; resourceHash: string; cacheSnapshotHash: string;
   195	    envelopeHash: string; eventLogPath: string;
   196	  };
   197	}
   198	
   199	interface BatteryState {
   200	  preflight: PreflightState;
   201	  semantic: {
   202	    topic: UnknownOr<Topic>; intent: UnknownOr<Intent>; binding: UnknownOr<Binding>;
   203	    presuppositions: UnknownOr<Presupposition[]>; answerRule: UnknownOr<AnswerRule>;
   204	    prior: UnknownOr<Prior>; settlementAct: UnknownOr<SettlementAct>;
   205	    questionType: UnknownOr<QuestionType>; domain: UnknownOr<Domain>;
   206	    vantages: UnknownOr<Vantage[]>; splitDecision: UnknownOr<SplitDecision>;
   207	  };
   208	  ledgers: {
   209	    queries: QueryRow[]; sources: SourceRow[]; absences: AbsenceRow[];
   210	    unknowns: UnknownRow[]; runs: RunRow[]; attempts: AttemptRow[];
   211	    claims: ClaimRow[]; provenance: ProvenanceRow[]; objections: ObjectionRow[];
   212	    beliefs: BeliefEvent[]; outcomes: OutcomeRow[]; liveness: LivenessRow[];
   213	  };
   214	  stageArtifacts: Record<string, ArtifactRef[]>;
   215	  gates: GateResult[];
   216	  events: AppendOnlyEvent[];
   217	}
   218	```
   219	
   220	Every prompt compiler MUST select only the fields named by its row contract. Auditor-only hashes, unrelated sources, prior model reasoning, and already resolved ledgers stay out of the prompt. Research and critic projections MUST be disjoint in context history; the critic receives claims, opened spans, runnable computations, receipt metadata, and open issues—not the researcher's chain of reasoning.
   221	
   222	### 1.5 Cache keys and invalidation
   223	
   224	| Artifact | Minimum cache key | Invalidate when |
   225	|---|---|---|
   226	| Question envelope | `hash(original) + caller scope + as_of + policy_version` | Any component changes. |
   227	| Query plan | `binding_id + type/domain/vantage versions + query policy` | Binding, registry, vantage, or amendment policy changes. |
   228	| Retrieved source | `canonical_locator + immutable source version/hash + retrieval adapter version` | Source version/hash or parser changes; age policy may mark stale without deleting bytes. |
   229	| Quote span | `source_hash + exact offsets/span hash + extractor version` | Source or extractor changes. |
   230	| Provenance cluster | `member source hashes + cluster algorithm version` | Membership metadata or algorithm changes. |
   231	| Instrument receipt | `instrument version + environment hash + fixture hashes` | Any dependency, environment, or fixture changes. |
   232	| Computation | `input artifact hashes + operator/version + code version` | Any input/operator/code changes. |
   233	| Critic receipt | `packet_hash + critic lineage identity + lineage policy version + context receipt` | Packet or lineage policy changes; never reuse as critique of a changed packet. |
   234	| Served answer | `all load-bearing artifact hashes + serve policy + evidence cutoff` | Any dependency, policy, cutoff, objection, or resolver state changes. |
