===== humans 181-303 =====
   181	The battery decomposes into eleven stages; each stage decomposes into the numbered
   182	questions below. `·A·` reproduces the source plan's always-run marker (read as
   183	"marked always-run in the source", not "fires on every question" — seven of these
   184	are conditional in their own text; see the activation notes). Where a question
   185	fires on a trigger, the trigger is stated.
   186	
   187	### Stage 1 — LOCK. Pin down the question, before any searching (Q1–Q6)
   188	
   189	- **Q1** ·A· What is this person really asking me — and what would they do differently depending on what I find?
   190	- **Q2** ·A· What exactly am I looking into — and what am I deliberately leaving out?
   191	- **Q3** ·A· What is this question taking for granted — and is any of it actually wrong?
   192	- **Q4** ·A· Before I look: what would I have to see to call this a yes, and what would make it a no?
   193	- **Q5** ·A· Before I look anything up: what do I already think the answer is, and how sure am I?
   194	- **Q6** ·A· Can I actually do this with the time and access I have — and how bad is it if I come back with "I don't know"?
   195	
   196	### Stage 2 — ROUTE. Decide what kind of question this is (Q7–Q10)
   197	
   198	- **Q7** ·A· What would actually settle this?
   199	- **Q8** ·A· What kind of question is this — and what do I need before I'm allowed to answer it?
   200	- **Q9** What else could be true here — and what one thing would I have to see to rule something out? *(fires: when more than one answer is still alive)*
   201	- **Q10** ·A· Do I actually need to break this into smaller questions, or can I just answer it?
   202	
   203	### Stage 3 — AIM. Write the search plan (Q11–Q14)
   204	
   205	- **Q11** ·A· What exactly am I going to type into the search box — including the words somebody would use to say the opposite?
   206	- **Q12** ·A· What don't I know yet that I'd need to know — and can I actually find it out?
   207	- **Q13** ·A· Who would actually know this — and what does each of them stand to gain from the answer going one way?
   208	- **Q14** Who is going to try to tear this apart when I'm done — and what would count as them landing a hit? *(fires: when a second checker is available)*
   209	
   210	### Stage 4 — HARVEST. Actually go and search (Q15–Q19)
   211	
   212	- **Q15** ·A· Did I actually run the searches I said I would — and what did each one turn up, including the ones that turned up nothing?
   213	- **Q16** ·A· Did I actually open this, or am I going on the snippet — and is this the original work or somebody's summary of it?
   214	- **Q17** ·A· What did I go looking for and fail to find?
   215	- **Q18** Is my newest source actually recent enough — and is this the kind of answer that goes stale? *(fires: when the answer could change over time)*
   216	- **Q19** Are these really separate sources, or the same people and the same data wearing different hats? *(fires: when there's more than one source)*
   217	
   218	### Stage 5 — RUN. Measure something yourself (Q20–Q25)
   219	
   220	Stage law: if a claim can be measured with the resources on hand, an unmeasured
   221	assertion of it is inadmissible; a skip is recorded and downgrades the answer to
   222	documents-only.
   223	
   224	- **Q20** ·A· What is the smallest, cheapest thing I could actually run or check myself that would move this answer?
   225	- **Q21** ·A· Before I run it: what do I expect to see, and what result would tell me I'm wrong? *(always, once something is actually being run)*
   226	- **Q22** ·A· What exactly did I run, and what exactly came back? *(always, once something is actually being run)*
   227	- **Q23** Does this tool actually work — does it say yes when the answer is yes, and no when the answer is no? *(fires: when relying on a tool or test)*
   228	- **Q24** Did I keep the attempts that went wrong, including the ones that make me look bad? *(fires: when something was actually measured)*
   229	- **Q25** If I can't run anything at all — what would it take, and who can say yes to it? *(fires: when nothing could be run)*
   230	
   231	### Stage 6 — SPLIT. Break the question apart, if that was justified (Q26–Q31)
   232	
   233	The whole stage runs only when Q10 decided to split; its ·A· marks mean "always,
   234	within a stage that may not happen". The generate/filter loop carries a hard cap
   235	declared at Q6.
   236	
   237	- **Q26** ·A· What would all have to be true for this to hold — and what one thing would sink it? *(children AND defeaters, produced in one act)*
   238	- **Q27** ·A· What part of the original question am I simply not covering?
   239	- **Q28** ·A· Could somebody who never saw the original question answer this piece on its own?
   240	- **Q29** ·A· What would I have to see to call this piece false — and how big would that difference have to be?
   241	- **Q30** ·A· If this piece turned out the other way, would it actually change my answer?
   242	- **Q31** Would somebody else — genuinely somebody else, not me in a different mood — have carved this up the same way? *(fires: when a split was made)*
   243	
   244	### Stage 7 — WEIGH. Weigh each piece of evidence (Q32–Q38)
   245	
   246	- **Q32** ·A· Is this evidence actually about my question, or just about something that sounds like it?
   247	- **Q33** ·A· What's the strongest thing I actually found that argues against me — not the strongest thing I can imagine?
   248	- **Q34** Am I holding the evidence against me to the same standard as the evidence for me? *(fires: when there's evidence on both sides)*
   249	- **Q35** Would this source be saying this even if it weren't true — and what do they get out of it? *(fires: when a source is carrying real weight)*
   250	- **Q36** ·A· This certainty I feel — did I measure it, or am I just feeling it?
   251	- **Q37** What could have gone wrong in this particular study to push its result the wrong way? *(fires: for cause-and-effect and measurement questions)*
   252	- **Q38** Where is the uncertainty in this number actually coming from? *(fires: when about to give a number)*
   253	
   254	### Stage 8 — CROSS. Have an AI built by someone else attack the work (Q39–Q44)
   255	
   256	Stage law: research and criticism never share a context; the agent that produced
   257	an artifact never grades it.
   258	
   259	- **Q39** ·A· Has somebody genuinely independent gone through this — before they knew what I concluded?
   260	- **Q40** ·A· Did the checker actually open my sources and redo my sums, or just read what I said about them?
   261	- **Q41** ·A· Can the checker point to something specific I got wrong — or at least say exactly what they looked at?
   262	- **Q42** When the checker agreed with me, had they already seen my reasoning? *(fires: when the checker agrees)*
   263	- **Q43** Did the checker try it their own way — and does my answer survive that? *(fires: when the question was split or pieces were combined)*
   264	- **Q44** ·A· Which objections have I actually dealt with — and is anything still standing?
   265	
   266	### Stage 9 — COMPOSE. Put the pieces back together (Q45–Q50)
   267	
   268	- **Q45** ·A· How am I putting these pieces together into one answer — and does the way I add them up change what comes out?
   269	- **Q46** ·A· Which single piece is really carrying this answer — and is it the one I checked hardest?
   270	- **Q47** ·A· If I'd combined these the other way, would I be giving the opposite answer?
   271	- **Q48** ·A· If I'd just answered this straight off, without all the breaking-down, would I have said the same thing?
   272	- **Q49** ·A· How fragile is this? What would I have to drop or change before the answer flips?
   273	- **Q50** Am I calling one option the winner just because of how I weighted things — and who decided those weights anyway? *(fires: when comparing options or judging a design)*
   274	
   275	### Stage 10 — SERVE. Write the answer (Q51–Q58)
   276	
   277	- **Q51** ·A· Can I show where all of this came from, and how I know each part? *(never switched off, for any question of any kind)*
   278	- **Q52** ·A· Does my first sentence answer the question they actually asked, and nothing bigger?
   279	- **Q53** ·A· Is the strongest objection right there where they'll see it — or buried where it can't hurt me?
   280	- **Q54** ·A· Did what I found actually change my mind — and if it did, was it the evidence that moved me?
   281	- **Q55** ·A· What am I still not sure about — and which kind of not-sure is it?
   282	- **Q56** Am I saying "I don't know" more often than I'm allowed to? *(fires: when somebody has said what "I don't know" costs)*
   283	- **Q57** Have I kept what I found separate from what I think should be done about it? *(fires: when a recommendation crept in)*
   284	- **Q58** ·A· What would have to happen for this answer to be wrong tomorrow?
   285	
   286	### Stage 11 — SETTLE. Come back and score it (Q59–Q62)
   287	
   288	- **Q59** ·A· When will we actually find out whether I was right — and who decides that, other than me?
   289	- **Q60** Have I written down what I said, how sure I was, and when we'll know — somewhere somebody else can find it? *(fires: when something will eventually settle it)*
   290	- **Q61** Was I right — and what should that change about how I answer questions like this? *(fires: when that day arrives)*
   291	- **Q62** ·A· When I got it wrong, where exactly did it go wrong?
   292	
   293	### The nine human-set rules (R1–R9)
   294	
   295	- **R1** Derive the search terms from the question itself — no retrieval runs on a query not derived here. *(Stage 3, Q11)*
   296	- **R2** Define the subject; evidence not about it is inadmissible. *(set at Q2, enforced at Q32)*
   297	- **R3** State what you do not yet know. *(Stage 3, Q12)*
   298	- **R4** Name who or where holds the answer. *(Stage 3, Q13)*
   299	- **R5** Research first, then critique — by a different lineage. *(all of Stage 8, plus Q14)*
   300	- **R6** Say what the question is about, in one plain sentence a stranger could route. *(before Q2)*
   301	- **R7** Say which field this belongs to, and which evidence standards that activates. *(beside Q8)*
   302	- **R8** Say from whose vantage points this should be answered. *(feeds Q13 and the critic assignment; never a rule for splitting the argument)*
   303	- **R9** The stranger test: a reader who knows nothing must be able to say back the answer, the certainty, and what would change it. *(Stage 10; blocks serving)*

